  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Trametes ochracea, commonly known as the Ochre Bracket, is a small to medium-sized shelf fungus that grows on hardwood logs and stumps throughout New England. This wood-decay fungus plays an important ecological role by breaking down dead wood and returning nutrients to forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Ochre to tan coloring",
      "description": "The upper surface displays distinctive ochre, tan, or buff coloring that may fade with age or exposure to weather.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore surface",
      "description": "The underside features small, angular pores (typically 3-5 per millimeter) that are pale yellow to whitish and may stain when bruised.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin, shelf-like fruiting body",
      "description": "Forms thin, bracket-shaped structures (usually 2-8 cm wide) that project from wood surfaces with a somewhat delicate appearance.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ochre Bracket typically grows on dead or decaying hardwoods, particularly oak, beech, and other deciduous trees in moist woodlands and forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other Trametes species, the Ochre Bracket produces powerful enzymes that can break down the complex polymers in wood, making it an important decomposer in forest nutrient cycling.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
